dership of Prophet Muhammad inspired Nusaybah bint Ka'ab's profound devotion and willingness to fight for the Islamic cause."}, {"sourceSlug": "nusaybah-bint-kaab", "sourceName": "Nusaybah bint Ka'ab", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "ridda-wars", "targetName": "Ridda Wars", "context": "Nusaybah bint Ka'ab fought in the Ridda Wars, notably at the Battle of Yamama in 632 CE, where she sustained severe injuries."}], "places": [{"name": "Medina, Saudi Arabia", "role": "Home Base, Early Islamic Center"}, {"name": "Mount Uhud, Saudi Arabia", "role": "Battle Site"}, {"name": "Al-Yamama, Saudi Arabia", "role": "Battle Site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Nusaybah bint Ka'ab", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "nusaybah-bint-kaab"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q4115264", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nusaybah_bint_Ka%27ab"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Nusaybah bint Ka'ab's direct participation and valor in early Islamic battles, particularly Uhud, established her as a powerful symbol of female courage and devotion. Her actions provided an enduring model for women's active engagement in religious and community life, influencing Islamic discourse on gender roles for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-15T07:34:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43688623340", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Naseebah bint Ka'ab, commonly known as Nusaybah bint Ka'ab. Her name is Naseebah bint Kaʿab ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf ibn Mabdhul ibn ʿAmr ibn Ghanm ibn Māzin ibn al-Najjār, and she is the mother of Ḥabīb and ʿAbdullāh, the two sons of Zayd ibn ʿĀṣim.", "newValue": "Nusaybah bint Ka'ab, an esteemed female companion of Prophet Muhammad, emerged as a pivotal figure in early Islamic history, renowned for her unwavering courage and direct participation in several critical battles. Born into the Banu Najjar clan in Medina, her life, though sparsely documented before her conversion, became inextricably linked with the nascent Muslim community's struggles from the Hijra (622 CE) until her death around 634 CE. Her unique role as a combatant, particularly at the Battle of Uhud, solidified her status as a paragon of female devotion and martial prowess within Islamic tradition, transcending typical gender roles of the era.

Her most celebrated actions occurred during the Battle of Uhud in 625 CE, a significant engagement where the Muslim forces faced a severe setback. As many male warriors retreated, Nusaybah bint Ka'ab, initially providing water to the wounded, famously took up arms to defend Prophet Muhammad directly. She shielded him with her body, enduring multiple sword and arrow wounds, notably a deep gash to her shoulder. Her valor was also evident in subsequent campaigns, including the Battle of Khaybar (628 CE), the Battle of Hunayn (630 CE), and the Battle of Yamama (632 CE) during the Ridda Wars, where she fought alongside her son, Abdullah ibn Zayd, and reportedly lost a hand.

The legacy of Nusaybah bint Ka'ab profoundly shaped the perception of women's roles in Islamic history and society. Her deeds established a powerful archetype of female resilience, piety, and active participation in the defense of faith and community, demonstrating that courage and devotion were not exclusive to men. She became an enduring symbol of the strength and commitment of the Sahabiyat (female companions of the Prophet), inspiring generations of Muslim women to engage actively in religious and societal spheres. Her story is frequently recounted in Islamic literature to exemplify the highest virtues of faith and bravery."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-15T07:34:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43688623340", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "Classical"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}], "newValue": ["The early struggles for survival of the nascent Muslim community in Medina necessitated broad participation in defense, including women.", "The direct threat posed by the Quraysh of Mecca to Prophet Muhammad and his followers compelled individuals like Nusaybah to take up arms.", "The teachings of Islam instilled a deep sense of devotion and willingness to sacrifice for faith among its early adherents."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-15T07:34:18Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#43688623340", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "634 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "634 CE"}], "newValue": ["Nusaybah bint Ka'ab's actions established a powerful archetype of female resilience and devotion within Islamic tradition.", "Her direct defense of Prop